@@ -58,6 +58,13 @@ Taixin TXW8301 (TXW4002A, C-SKY CK803) HaLow wireless-bridge (WNB) firmware SDK
   - A station only searches the listed frequencies.
 - WiFi modes: `WIFI_WNBAP_SUPPORT` and `WIFI_WNBSTA_SUPPORT` are 1 in `project_config.h`. WNB is Taixin's own bridge protocol; a WNB AP only accepts WNB stations, and the WNB STA has roaming enabled. The UI sends the side as `wifi_mode` (1 STA, 2 AP, 3 repeater) and the type as `wifi_wnb` (0 standard, 1 WNB), a firmware extension. `webui.c` merges the two (`webui_mode_make`). A saved mode change reboots the device, because the interfaces, ACS, the DHCP server and the role pin are only set up at boot. `device_cfg` has a 10th field, `wnbEn`. Extensions to the vendor replies: `conn_state` adds tx MCS, rx MCS (station side, towards the AP; -1 otherwise), `bss_bw`, tx KiB and rx KiB; each `stalist` entry adds tx MCS, rx MCS, `bss_bw`, tx KiB and rx KiB (`ieee80211_conf_get_stainfo`, `webui_peer_info`). Whether the umac fills `tx_bytes`/`rx_bytes` was not verified on hardware yet. The UI converts MCS 0–10 + bandwidth to a PHY rate (`linkRateText` in `halowPlan.ts`, 1 SS, long GI) and shows only the RX rate. The TX MCS always reads 0 on hardware: the closed lmac does not implement `LMAC_IOCTL_GET_STA_TX_MCS` (it falls through to the default case of the `lmac_get_cfg` jump table), so no per-station TX rate is available. An unexpected RX value is shown as a raw `MCS n`. Repeater needs `WIFI_REPEATER_SUPPORT=1`, which changes `struct sys_config`, so all upgraded devices reset to defaults. It is not enabled.
 
+## Heap (open, 2026-11-20)
+
+- Status on hardware: system heap ~8 KB free of 60 KB when idle; after a Device log session it stayed at ~1.5 KB. Packet buffers (SKB pool) 86.8 of 96.5 KB free, so the SKB pool has room to give.
+- Split (`sys_config.h`): `SYS_HEAP_SIZE` 60 KB (`#ifndef`, can be overridden in `project_config.h`), `WIFI_RX_BUFF_SIZE` 64 KB, SKB pool = the rest up to `__heap_end` 0x200b0000 (`skbpool_init` in `main.c`). Moving heap from the SKB pool is the likely fix, but the boot failures (see Parked ideas) may come from a minimum SKB pool size (~98 KB), so test e.g. `SYS_HEAP_SIZE (64*1024)` on its own and watch boot.
+- Ruled out in `webui.c`: request/reply buffers are freed on every path; the log tail uses a static ring. lwIP PCBs come from static memp pools. `sys_dbginfo_print`/`AT+SYSDBG heap 1` print from the stack.
+- Remaining suspects: `g_at_buf` (2 KB, `WEBUI_AT_BUF`) is allocated on the first web AT command and never freed, by design so far; lwIP send data (`TCP_SND_BUF` 4×1460, from the heap via `MEM_LIBC_MALLOC=1`) held by connections still retransmitting after 1 s polling over WiFi; allocations in the closed libs. Next step: check whether heap recovers after a few idle minutes, and capture one `AT+SYSDBG heap 1` dump through the Device log (it lists allocations by function and line).
+
